{"dir":"out","ms":5865,"line":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"method":"session/prompt","params":{"sessionId":"2040ed09-9cf2-4231-a2d5-d0371f731efc","prompt":[{"type":"text","text":"The workspace is `{{CWD}}`, not the directory you start in: name its files by their full path there.\n\nThis task is read-only: your own file edits, shell and web tools are off. Read files, search the repository and run tests with `run_command`, read web pages with `fetch` and search the web with `web_search`.\n\nWhen you have fully completed this task, call the `task_complete` tool with status `done` and your handoff. If you cannot complete it, call `task_complete` with status `blocked` or `failed` and the reason instead. Call it once, as your last action.\n\nIts `summary` is this task's handoff to the next task: the planner and the tasks that depend on this one read it first, and nothing else you write reaches them unless they ask. Write it for that reader, not as a log: what changed and the decisions behind it; the interfaces and paths a dependent relies on; the assumptions you acted on; what is left or open. It is at most 1500 characters, and a call over a limit is refused: the long form goes in `report`.\n\nPut anything the next reader must act on in `findings`, one item each, most important first, each with a severity, a one-line title, its detail and evidence to check it by: a file (and line), a commit, a task (and one of its findings, as \"F3\") or a URL. Ordewell numbers them F1, F2, … so a later task can cite one. List what you produced in `artifacts`.\n\nWork from your brief and the prior task outputs you were given; pull more with `read` only when you need it, and read the code your task touches, not the whole repository.\n\nThe long story, if there is one, goes in `report`: it is kept with this attempt and read only on demand. A review or other read-only task especially: its findings are its result, so state each one, and do not write the report to a file.\n\nThis task is read-only and its worktree is thrown away when it ends. If you build a prototype there, keep it with the `save_patch` tool before you finish: a later task can then apply it.\n\nRead the web with the `fetch` and `web_search` tools: your runner's own web tools are off on this task, and these work whether or not your commands run sealed. Nobody is asked: a read the user's web-read settings do not already allow is refused, saying why. Do not retry a refusal; report what you could not read in your handoff.\n\nread {{CWD}}/README.md and complete with the secret word it names as the summary."}]}}}
